@title Member-Profile Database
@subtitle Database choice for the new member-profile service
@author Accounts team, Lattice Fitness
@date Decision due 2026-11-04

kpi Profiles | 9 million | ~2 KB each
kpi Peak reads | 3,500/s
kpi Peak writes | 220/s

The workload is mostly key lookups, plus a few admin searches by email and gym. We compared PostgreSQL (RDS), DynamoDB and MongoDB Atlas.

## Comparison
table = Option,Query flexibility,Scaling model,Ops effort,Multi-region; PostgreSQL (RDS),High,Vertical plus read replicas,Medium,Read replicas only; DynamoDB,Low,Automatic,Low,Global tables; MongoDB Atlas,Medium,Sharding,Medium,Global clusters

## Cost at scale
data cost = traffic,PostgreSQL,DynamoDB,MongoDB Atlas; Today,780,540,910; 3x traffic,1640,1420,1870; 10x traffic,4300,4650,5200
chart bar cost x=traffic unit=$ title="Estimated monthly cost by traffic level"

DynamoDB is cheapest today and at 3x; PostgreSQL overtakes it at 10x.

## Pros and cons
### PostgreSQL (RDS)
- Pro: flexible queries for admin search
- Pro: cheapest at 10x traffic
- Con: scales vertically, with read replicas
- Con: multi-region is read replicas only
### DynamoDB
- Pro: automatic scaling and low ops effort
- Pro: global tables
- Pro: cheapest today and at 3x
- Con: poor fit for ad hoc admin search
### MongoDB Atlas
- Pro: medium query flexibility
- Pro: global clusters
- Con: most expensive at every traffic level
- Con: sharding to operate

## Recommendation
! **Choose PostgreSQL.** Admin search needs flexible queries, and at our realistic 3x horizon its cost stays within $220 a month of DynamoDB.

!! If write volume rises above 2,000 per second, we will have to revisit sharding.
